# Commits that changed formatting without changing behaviour, so `git blame`
# can skip past them to the change that actually wrote the line.
#
# GitHub reads this file automatically. Locally it does nothing until you point
# git at it, which is once per clone:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# Full 40-character hashes only — git rejects an abbreviated one, and rejects a
# hash it cannot resolve, so a stale entry here fails loudly rather than
# quietly ignoring nothing.
#
# Add a commit here only if reverting it would change no behaviour. The entry
# below is not purely mechanical and is listed anyway: alongside the 1,128-file
# rewrite it moved two `# noqa` directives onto the lines they waive, dropped a
# `# type: ignore` that was concealing a real return-type mismatch, and
# corrected line-numbered suppression entries whose sites had shifted. Blame on
# those few lines skips to whoever last wrote them, which is the more useful
# answer in each case — they changed *because of* the reformat, not on their
# own merits.
#
# It did not catch all of them. A fourth suppression, in a package suite this
# commit did not run, was repaired in a follow-up rather than folded back in
# here — the reformat is what moved it, so blame on that line wants the same
# treatment, but rewriting the commit would have erased the more useful record
# that a formatting change breaks suppressions in four different ways.

